WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:01.085
- Hi everyone.

00:00:01.085 --> 00:00:03.362
Welcome to the Homeroom live stream.

00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:05.682
Sal Khan here.

00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:06.902
Very exciting guest today.

00:00:06.902 --> 00:00:08.112
We're gonna have Vivek Murthy,

00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:11.052
former surgeon general
of the United States.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:13.932
Obviously, there's a lot of
things to talk about health

00:00:13.932 --> 00:00:15.552
and mental health related right now

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:17.212
in the world for better, for worse.

00:00:17.212 --> 00:00:18.392
But before we get into that,

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:19.882
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:21.942
First of all, a reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:21.942 --> 00:00:23.842
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:23.842 --> 00:00:25.372
We can only exist through donations

00:00:25.372 --> 00:00:26.522
from folks like yourself.

00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:28.232
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:32.002
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:32.002 --> 00:00:33.922
And as we saw several guests ago

00:00:33.922 --> 00:00:35.672
with the professor from Yale,

00:00:35.672 --> 00:00:36.782
by donating to Khan Academy

00:00:36.782 --> 00:00:38.642
not only will you be helping many people,

00:00:38.642 --> 00:00:40.962
but you yourself will
become a happier person

00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:43.112
that's what research tells us.

00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:45.022
I also wanna give a special shout out

00:00:45.022 --> 00:00:47.252
to several organizations
that have stepped up.

00:00:47.252 --> 00:00:49.852
And I'm guessing they're
now happier organizations

00:00:49.852 --> 00:00:52.062
because they have stepped
up to support Khan Academy

00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:53.532
especially during COVID,

00:00:53.532 --> 00:00:55.042
when we were already running a deficit

00:00:55.042 --> 00:00:56.462
and our deficits grew,

00:00:56.462 --> 00:00:59.112
and we continue to run deficits for 2021.

00:00:59.112 --> 00:01:01.812
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:01.812 --> 00:01:03.872
AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis,

00:01:03.872 --> 00:01:06.472
and the many, many other
supporters of Khan Academy.

00:01:07.352 --> 00:01:09.532
Last announcement, just a reminder

00:01:09.532 --> 00:01:12.872
that you can get a version of
what you're hearing right now

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:16.152
as a podcast "Homeroom
with Sal" the podcast

00:01:16.152 --> 00:01:18.572
wherever you get podcasts.

00:01:18.572 --> 00:01:20.872
Now with that, I'm excited
to go into our conversation.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:23.722
We have Vivek Murthy, the
former surgeon general

00:01:23.722 --> 00:01:25.842
and now author of "Together".

00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:28.422
The Healing Power of Human Connection

00:01:28.422 --> 00:01:31.952
in a Sometimes Lonely World.

00:01:31.952 --> 00:01:34.035
Vivek, thanks for joining us.

00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:36.892
- Hey Sal, it's great to join you.

00:01:36.892 --> 00:01:37.792
How are you today?

00:01:39.694 --> 00:01:41.112
- I'm doing all right.

00:01:41.112 --> 00:01:42.702
I mean, there's so much I
wanna talk to you about.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:43.932
Maybe the first thing,

00:01:43.932 --> 00:01:47.772
I mean, just the role surgeon general.

00:01:47.772 --> 00:01:49.842
I remember when I was a kid,

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.852
and I remember that's what C. Everett Koop

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:52.902
was the surgeon general.

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:54.672
And he had the whole no smoking campaign

00:01:54.672 --> 00:01:56.362
which was great, but I was just like,

00:01:56.362 --> 00:01:59.884
is he a surgeon or is he a
general or is he neither?

00:01:59.884 --> 00:02:00.772
(Vivek laughs)

00:02:00.772 --> 00:02:03.872
So explain to folks what
the surgeon general is,

00:02:03.872 --> 00:02:06.375
and why is it called surgeon general?

00:02:07.942 --> 00:02:09.242
- Well, it's a great question.

00:02:09.242 --> 00:02:10.702
So the surgeon general

00:02:11.602 --> 00:02:13.322
is an individual in the US government

00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:15.052
who has two roles.

00:02:15.052 --> 00:02:18.122
One is to number one, provide the public

00:02:18.122 --> 00:02:20.002
with the best information on health

00:02:20.002 --> 00:02:22.692
so people can make good
decisions for their families.

00:02:22.692 --> 00:02:25.072
And the other role is to lead

00:02:25.072 --> 00:02:27.262
one of the uniformed
services in the US Government

00:02:27.262 --> 00:02:30.392
called the US Public Health
Service Commissioned Corps.

00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:33.622
And this is a group of about
6,600 uniformed officers,

00:02:33.622 --> 00:02:36.762
doctors, nurses, physical therapists

00:02:36.762 --> 00:02:38.722
and public health engineers and others,

00:02:38.722 --> 00:02:41.002
who each and every day are
working in federal agencies

00:02:41.002 --> 00:02:41.962
to strengthen public health

00:02:41.962 --> 00:02:44.522
and also deploy during times of emergency

00:02:44.522 --> 00:02:47.462
to help at tornadoes and
the aftermath of hurricanes.

00:02:47.462 --> 00:02:50.392
We sent them to Liberia to
help with the ebola outbreak

00:02:51.466 --> 00:02:52.322
a few years back.

00:02:52.322 --> 00:02:55.442
And together, the surgeon general manages

00:02:55.442 --> 00:02:56.702
these two responsibilities.

00:02:56.702 --> 00:02:59.962
But it's because of the role
leading the Commissioned Corps,

00:02:59.962 --> 00:03:01.582
one of our uniformed services,

00:03:01.582 --> 00:03:04.272
that is actually why the
surgeon general wears a uniform.

00:03:04.272 --> 00:03:05.792
And lastly, I'll tell you
that the surgeon general

00:03:05.792 --> 00:03:10.322
is actually not a
general in terms of rank,

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:13.052
because the Public Health
Service Commissioned Corps

00:03:13.052 --> 00:03:14.612
follows the Navy rank system.

00:03:14.612 --> 00:03:17.812
So then sergeant general is
actually a three-star admiral

00:03:17.812 --> 00:03:20.555
as opposed to a general per say.

00:03:22.062 --> 00:03:24.622
- But they don't call
it the surgeon admiral.

00:03:24.622 --> 00:03:25.612
- But they don't call
it the surgeon admiral

00:03:25.612 --> 00:03:28.002
and it turns out that you
don't have to be a surgeon

00:03:28.002 --> 00:03:31.202
to be the surgeon general. (laughs)

00:03:31.202 --> 00:03:33.492
In fact, most of the
recent surgeons general,

00:03:33.492 --> 00:03:37.062
including myself, have been
either internal medicine doctors

00:03:37.062 --> 00:03:38.402
or family medicine doctors.

00:03:38.402 --> 00:03:40.382
There have been surgeons, Dr. Carmona

00:03:40.382 --> 00:03:42.602
who is George W. Bush's surgeon general

00:03:42.602 --> 00:03:43.622
was a trauma surgeon.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:46.145
C Everett Koop was a pediatric surgeon.

00:03:47.632 --> 00:03:51.802
- So with your former
role as surgeon general

00:03:51.802 --> 00:03:53.872
and now this book that
you're writing, "Together"

00:03:53.872 --> 00:03:54.932
and it seems like this touches

00:03:54.932 --> 00:03:57.172
on kind of social-emotional wellbeing,

00:03:57.172 --> 00:03:58.522
although I'll let you describe it.

00:03:58.522 --> 00:04:00.002
I'm sure you can do a better job.

00:04:00.002 --> 00:04:01.882
I mean, it seems like
one of your focus areas

00:04:01.882 --> 00:04:03.192
really has been

00:04:03.192 --> 00:04:06.582
not just on the traditional
physical health side of things

00:04:06.582 --> 00:04:08.152
as surgeon general,
but also in the mental.

00:04:08.152 --> 00:04:09.492
I mean, well actually I
don't wanna speak for you.

00:04:09.492 --> 00:04:12.702
What have been your priorities
when you were surgeon general

00:04:12.702 --> 00:04:14.232
and now what type of change

00:04:14.232 --> 00:04:17.192
or information are you
trying to get out there?

00:04:17.192 --> 00:04:18.962
- Well, you know, when I
was surgeon general, Sal,

00:04:18.962 --> 00:04:21.982
I began my time in office
with a listening tour

00:04:21.982 --> 00:04:23.962
where I just spend time
traveling around the country,

00:04:23.962 --> 00:04:25.702
asking people how I could help.

00:04:25.702 --> 00:04:28.272
And the issues that I heard from them

00:04:28.272 --> 00:04:31.522
were consistent with what we
are hearing and seeing a lot.

00:04:31.522 --> 00:04:34.292
And later what I was seeing
in the hospital as a doctor

00:04:34.292 --> 00:04:36.342
which is that the fundamental problem

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:38.402
that we have with the opioid epidemic

00:04:38.402 --> 00:04:40.942
was affecting communities
all across the country.

00:04:40.942 --> 00:04:43.772
The emerging challenge with
e-cigarettes at that time

00:04:43.772 --> 00:04:46.922
was also presenting a great
challenge in terms of,

00:04:46.922 --> 00:04:48.972
you know, clawing back in
fact some of the progress

00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:52.132
we had made on tobacco and smoking.

00:04:52.132 --> 00:04:53.682
And so I focus a lot on those

00:04:53.682 --> 00:04:55.392
but the issue around mental health

00:04:55.392 --> 00:04:58.492
and particularly around
loneliness and social connection

00:04:58.492 --> 00:05:01.602
came about in part because
of these early conversations.

00:05:01.602 --> 00:05:03.622
And because I realized that

00:05:03.622 --> 00:05:05.952
behind so many of the
stories I was hearing,

00:05:05.952 --> 00:05:08.852
stories around chronic
illness, around depression,

00:05:08.852 --> 00:05:11.812
around addiction were
these threads of loneliness

00:05:11.812 --> 00:05:13.309
where people would often say to me,

00:05:13.309 --> 00:05:15.912
"You know, I feel I have
to carry all these burdens

00:05:15.912 --> 00:05:17.622
in my life by myself.

00:05:17.622 --> 00:05:20.332
Or I feel if I disappeared tomorrow,

00:05:20.332 --> 00:05:22.872
nobody would even care,
they wouldn't even notice."

00:05:22.872 --> 00:05:24.472
People felt invisible.

00:05:24.472 --> 00:05:28.112
And it wasn't just the
people we stereotypically

00:05:28.112 --> 00:05:31.952
think of as lonely, perhaps
the older individual

00:05:31.952 --> 00:05:34.052
who's living at home by themselves.

00:05:34.052 --> 00:05:36.082
But I was hearing this
from college students, Sal

00:05:36.082 --> 00:05:39.202
who were surrounded by
thousands of other students

00:05:39.202 --> 00:05:41.322
on campus but still felt profoundly alone.

00:05:41.322 --> 00:05:43.342
I was hearing this from moms and dads

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:45.702
in neighborhoods where they
were connected to the school,

00:05:45.702 --> 00:05:46.762
connected to their neighbors

00:05:46.762 --> 00:05:50.112
but still didn't quite
feel like there was anybody

00:05:50.112 --> 00:05:51.392
that they could truly confide in,

00:05:51.392 --> 00:05:53.972
anyone who truly got
them and understood them.

00:05:53.972 --> 00:05:57.292
And so on it went whether
I was talking to CEOs

00:05:57.292 --> 00:05:58.802
or nurses and doctors

00:05:58.802 --> 00:06:00.402
or even members of Congress themselves.

00:06:00.402 --> 00:06:02.692
I came to realize that loneliness

00:06:02.692 --> 00:06:04.442
and this feeling of being isolated

00:06:04.442 --> 00:06:05.842
is extraordinarily common.

00:06:05.842 --> 00:06:08.252
And it turns out it has profound impacts

00:06:08.252 --> 00:06:10.852
and implications for our
physical and mental health.

00:06:12.402 --> 00:06:13.332
- That's fascinating.

00:06:13.332 --> 00:06:15.162
And I wanna encourage everyone watching

00:06:15.162 --> 00:06:17.292
put your questions on Facebook or YouTube.

00:06:17.292 --> 00:06:20.792
I'll surface as many of them
as we can to Dr. Murthy.

00:06:20.792 --> 00:06:23.532
But you know, on this
question of loneliness,

00:06:23.532 --> 00:06:25.672
we've had other guests,
who've talked about trends,

00:06:25.672 --> 00:06:29.262
social media making at least
young people feel more anxious

00:06:29.262 --> 00:06:30.962
or in many cases depressed.

00:06:30.962 --> 00:06:34.152
Is this in your mind a new phenomenon

00:06:34.152 --> 00:06:35.692
or has it always been there

00:06:35.692 --> 00:06:38.002
and we're just finally unpacking it?

00:06:38.002 --> 00:06:40.722
This loneliness phenomenon especially.

00:06:40.722 --> 00:06:43.942
- Well, loneliness has
been with us for ages,

00:06:43.942 --> 00:06:45.122
for centuries.

00:06:45.122 --> 00:06:47.332
And I mean, interestingly,
if you look back

00:06:47.332 --> 00:06:49.822
in the literature at
least in Western culture,

00:06:49.822 --> 00:06:51.842
Shakespeare was actually
one of the first people

00:06:51.842 --> 00:06:54.872
to actually talk
explicitly about loneliness

00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:56.805
in his work or to reference it.

00:06:57.702 --> 00:06:58.912
But people have been feeling lonely

00:06:58.912 --> 00:07:00.402
for thousands of years.

00:07:00.402 --> 00:07:02.302
But there are a couple of
things that are different now.

00:07:02.302 --> 00:07:07.242
I think one is that we,
unlike times in past

00:07:07.242 --> 00:07:10.432
where we tended to live in
the same place that we grew up

00:07:10.432 --> 00:07:13.152
where we tended to have
generations upon generations,

00:07:13.152 --> 00:07:14.462
living in close proximity

00:07:14.462 --> 00:07:16.802
and often living in the same household,

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:19.352
we are living in a very
different society now.

00:07:19.352 --> 00:07:22.392
One that is relatively new in
the history of civilization,

00:07:22.392 --> 00:07:23.802
where we are more mobile,

00:07:23.802 --> 00:07:27.542
where we don't necessarily live
in extended family networks.

00:07:27.542 --> 00:07:30.712
And the overlay of technology on that

00:07:30.712 --> 00:07:32.812
has been a mixed blessing.

00:07:32.812 --> 00:07:34.782
While it allows us to connect deeply

00:07:34.782 --> 00:07:36.272
with people and to stay
in touch with people

00:07:36.272 --> 00:07:39.892
we may not otherwise be
allowed to see as easily,

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:42.502
we also find that the way in
which we're using technology

00:07:42.502 --> 00:07:45.222
often displaces our in-person connection

00:07:45.222 --> 00:07:48.532
or dilutes our in-person conversations

00:07:48.532 --> 00:07:51.212
as we bring our devices, for
example, to the dinner table

00:07:51.212 --> 00:07:53.252
or we allow ourselves to be on them

00:07:53.252 --> 00:07:55.012
when we're catching up with friends.

00:07:55.012 --> 00:07:56.822
And then finally, there's
one last piece here

00:07:56.822 --> 00:07:57.655
that I worry about Sal,

00:07:57.655 --> 00:07:59.342
which is I think there's an element

00:07:59.342 --> 00:08:03.092
around how our current culture

00:08:03.092 --> 00:08:05.932
augmented by technology
but independent of that,

00:08:05.932 --> 00:08:09.732
how it actually contributes
to people's sense of self

00:08:09.732 --> 00:08:10.912
and to their own wellbeing.

00:08:10.912 --> 00:08:12.342
And what I worry about Sal

00:08:12.342 --> 00:08:16.782
is I think that for many of
our young people in particular

00:08:16.782 --> 00:08:17.922
but really for all of us.

00:08:17.922 --> 00:08:19.122
What our culture tells us

00:08:19.122 --> 00:08:23.042
is that we have meaning, we
have worth as human beings,

00:08:23.042 --> 00:08:24.532
if we're successful.

00:08:24.532 --> 00:08:27.922
And we're successful, if we can
acquire one of three things,

00:08:27.922 --> 00:08:31.272
wealth, power or fame.

00:08:31.272 --> 00:08:32.112
And if we acquire those

00:08:32.112 --> 00:08:34.282
then we were held up as success stories.

00:08:34.282 --> 00:08:36.532
And those are the people
we read about in the paper.

00:08:36.532 --> 00:08:39.292
Those are the people whose
biographies are lauded.

00:08:39.292 --> 00:08:41.772
Those are the people
who are seen as heroes.

00:08:41.772 --> 00:08:44.642
But the message that we
send to everyone in society

00:08:44.642 --> 00:08:48.152
is that if you're not
wealthy, powerful or famous,

00:08:48.152 --> 00:08:50.442
that you are not as worthy.

00:08:50.442 --> 00:08:53.042
And that is a fundamental problem

00:08:53.042 --> 00:08:55.882
because I think our fundamental
worth as human beings,

00:08:55.882 --> 00:08:57.772
is not extrinsic, it's intrinsic.

00:08:57.772 --> 00:09:00.392
It comes from our ability
to give and to receive

00:09:00.392 --> 00:09:02.552
love, compassion and kindness.

00:09:02.552 --> 00:09:03.872
And that's something we're born with.

00:09:03.872 --> 00:09:05.122
It's something I see my four year old

00:09:05.122 --> 00:09:07.912
and my two year old
doing each and every day

00:09:07.912 --> 00:09:08.972
because that's what kids do,

00:09:08.972 --> 00:09:10.702
that's how we're born into the world.

00:09:10.702 --> 00:09:13.352
But we forget it, other things
get layered on top of it

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:14.185
we come to believe

00:09:14.185 --> 00:09:16.432
that if we're kind we'll
get taken advantage of.

00:09:16.432 --> 00:09:17.802
That the world's a harsh place

00:09:17.802 --> 00:09:20.312
and that we need to pursue
these other objectives

00:09:20.312 --> 00:09:23.172
like work, wealth and
power to have meaning.

00:09:23.172 --> 00:09:24.362
And that's a challenge

00:09:24.362 --> 00:09:26.865
that's a particular
feature of modern society.

00:09:28.152 --> 00:09:30.212
- And is there anything
we can do about it?

00:09:30.212 --> 00:09:32.262
I mean, you know, we can hope

00:09:32.262 --> 00:09:36.532
that people somehow start profiling folks

00:09:36.532 --> 00:09:39.362
who have really content
family lives and all of that.

00:09:39.362 --> 00:09:41.682
But I don't think, you
know, TMZ is about to change

00:09:41.682 --> 00:09:45.832
or People Magazine is about
to change their coverage.

00:09:45.832 --> 00:09:47.982
Look at this really
content father of four.

00:09:47.982 --> 00:09:50.282
You've never heard of
him but he's very happy.

00:09:53.386 --> 00:09:54.692
How do we change this?

00:09:54.692 --> 00:09:55.942
I mean, if you can't change culture

00:09:55.942 --> 00:09:57.702
are there tools that we can do

00:10:00.282 --> 00:10:02.375
to mitigate the negative impacts?

00:10:03.462 --> 00:10:05.662
- Well this is what I actually
found very encouraging

00:10:05.662 --> 00:10:07.912
about this topic, Sal
is that you might think

00:10:07.912 --> 00:10:10.352
loneliness hmm that
sounds kind of depressing.

00:10:10.352 --> 00:10:11.332
It turns out though,

00:10:11.332 --> 00:10:14.252
that the reason I think
this is profoundly inspiring

00:10:14.252 --> 00:10:17.152
and hopeful area is because
the opposite of loneliness,

00:10:17.152 --> 00:10:18.732
which is social connection

00:10:18.732 --> 00:10:21.722
is something that we
are hardwired to pursue

00:10:21.722 --> 00:10:22.952
and to build into our lives.

00:10:22.952 --> 00:10:25.092
We feel better, frankly
when we're together.

00:10:25.092 --> 00:10:27.072
We feel safer when we're together.

00:10:27.072 --> 00:10:28.502
It doesn't mean we're all extroverts

00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:30.462
but what it means is that we all need

00:10:30.462 --> 00:10:32.842
some degree of social connection,
we're better off for it.

00:10:32.842 --> 00:10:35.432
And because we are hardwired in that way,

00:10:35.432 --> 00:10:37.722
it turns out that even a little bit

00:10:37.722 --> 00:10:39.542
of meaningful social interaction

00:10:39.542 --> 00:10:41.402
can actually make us
feel profoundly better

00:10:41.402 --> 00:10:42.802
for a long period of time.

00:10:42.802 --> 00:10:43.882
So just think for a moment

00:10:43.882 --> 00:10:47.342
about the last time perhaps
you were in the grocery store

00:10:47.342 --> 00:10:52.182
or in a coffee shop and
you passed on a kind word

00:10:52.182 --> 00:10:53.562
to the person at the cash register.

00:10:53.562 --> 00:10:55.582
Or when they actually
asked how you were doing

00:10:55.582 --> 00:10:59.302
and then paused to listen
to what the answer was.

00:10:59.302 --> 00:11:01.242
You know, those are moments
which make us feel good

00:11:01.242 --> 00:11:03.792
because they make us feel seen and heard.

00:11:03.792 --> 00:11:06.532
And they can land that feeling of...

00:11:06.532 --> 00:11:08.562
Feeling seen and heard can
last with us for hours.

00:11:08.562 --> 00:11:10.992
Similarly, when you
have a friend who calls.

00:11:10.992 --> 00:11:13.192
Let's say unexpectedly you get
a call from your best friend

00:11:13.192 --> 00:11:15.302
and you have a five or
10 minute conversation,

00:11:15.302 --> 00:11:16.582
that can lead you to feeling great

00:11:16.582 --> 00:11:18.222
for a long period of time.

00:11:18.222 --> 00:11:20.592
So the way we rebuild connected lives,

00:11:20.592 --> 00:11:23.712
the way we recenter
ourselves around a culture

00:11:23.712 --> 00:11:25.812
that recognizes our worth is intrinsic

00:11:25.812 --> 00:11:28.422
and grounded in our ability
to give and receive love,

00:11:28.422 --> 00:11:31.422
is we do that by leading by example.

00:11:31.422 --> 00:11:35.442
We do that by allowing ourselves
to in small ways initially,

00:11:35.442 --> 00:11:38.602
reach out to people and enjoy the fruits

00:11:38.602 --> 00:11:39.452
of human connection.

00:11:39.452 --> 00:11:41.042
And here's some simple ways we can do it.

00:11:41.042 --> 00:11:43.812
So like first for example,
we can keep 15 minutes

00:11:43.812 --> 00:11:46.332
a day aside to reach out to

00:11:46.332 --> 00:11:47.662
and connect with people we care about.

00:11:47.662 --> 00:11:50.222
It could be a phone call
or a video conference call.

00:11:50.222 --> 00:11:52.829
It could be writing to
somebody that we love saying,

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:53.842
"Hey, I'm just thinking of you

00:11:53.842 --> 00:11:55.112
and I wanna know how you are."

00:11:55.112 --> 00:11:55.945
Second thing we can do

00:11:55.945 --> 00:11:59.492
is we can make the time we
have with people really count

00:11:59.492 --> 00:12:01.122
by improving the quality of it.

00:12:01.122 --> 00:12:03.142
And the way we do that most readily

00:12:03.142 --> 00:12:06.352
is by eliminating distraction
when we're talking to others.

00:12:06.352 --> 00:12:09.332
That's our cell phone, that's our computer

00:12:09.332 --> 00:12:11.602
if we have a tendency to check email

00:12:11.602 --> 00:12:13.512
or the news while we're on the phone.

00:12:13.512 --> 00:12:15.862
And the third thing we can
do is seek each other out.

00:12:15.862 --> 00:12:17.732
Looking to serve one another.

00:12:17.732 --> 00:12:21.072
It turns out Sal from
ourselves to the other person.

00:12:21.072 --> 00:12:25.202
And we also reinforce to
ourselves and remind ourselves

00:12:25.202 --> 00:12:26.892
that we have value to bring to the world.

00:12:26.892 --> 00:12:28.342
And this is so important, Sal

00:12:28.342 --> 00:12:31.192
because when people become
lonely for a long period of time,

00:12:31.192 --> 00:12:32.962
what happens is they lose faith

00:12:32.962 --> 00:12:35.312
that they actually have any
value to bring to the world.

00:12:35.312 --> 00:12:36.492
They start to feel that they're lonely

00:12:36.492 --> 00:12:37.572
'cause they're not likable.

00:12:37.572 --> 00:12:39.132
And they also find that their focus

00:12:39.132 --> 00:12:41.192
ends up being excessively on themselves

00:12:41.192 --> 00:12:43.582
because they're worried
fundamentally about safety,

00:12:43.582 --> 00:12:46.492
which is what we all
worry about unconsciously

00:12:46.492 --> 00:12:47.392
when we're lonely.

00:12:48.472 --> 00:12:49.305
- Yeah

00:12:49.305 --> 00:12:50.138
No, that makes a ton of sense.

00:12:50.138 --> 00:12:51.782
And I mean, there's two
things that I just heard.

00:12:51.782 --> 00:12:54.272
Because when you kind
of reach out to someone

00:12:54.272 --> 00:12:56.232
and especially show
gratitude towards them,

00:12:56.232 --> 00:12:57.892
and this gels with our conversation

00:12:57.892 --> 00:13:00.452
we had a couple of days
ago, actually last week

00:13:00.452 --> 00:13:03.332
about the science of how
to be a happy person.

00:13:03.332 --> 00:13:05.602
You know, not only do you show that person

00:13:05.602 --> 00:13:07.332
that you see them, you appreciate them.

00:13:07.332 --> 00:13:09.612
They feel less lonely,
they feel more safe.

00:13:09.612 --> 00:13:12.762
But there's good research
that it makes you happier

00:13:12.762 --> 00:13:16.052
when you show that gratitude
towards other people.

00:13:16.052 --> 00:13:17.402
You know, question for you.

00:13:21.862 --> 00:13:24.272
You are accomplished in all
of the very traditional ways

00:13:24.272 --> 00:13:26.764
that we just mentioned that
we shouldn't highlight people

00:13:26.764 --> 00:13:27.597
(Sal laughs)

00:13:27.597 --> 00:13:30.312
But you are accomplished in those ways.

00:13:30.312 --> 00:13:35.312
What do you do to not lose your balance?

00:13:35.852 --> 00:13:37.762
You know, I'm sure when you
were part of the administration,

00:13:37.762 --> 00:13:40.422
you saw a lot of classically type A people

00:13:40.422 --> 00:13:42.602
who are work, work, work work.

00:13:42.602 --> 00:13:44.282
Probably not taking the 15 minutes

00:13:44.282 --> 00:13:45.919
to kind of call up their cousin and say,

00:13:45.919 --> 00:13:47.119
"Hey, how are you doing?

00:13:48.316 --> 00:13:49.149
What do you do?

00:13:49.149 --> 00:13:52.162
And how do you coach people
to try to balance things

00:13:52.162 --> 00:13:53.062
a little bit more?

00:13:54.542 --> 00:13:56.382
- Well, it's a great question, Sal.

00:13:56.382 --> 00:14:00.222
And let me tell you that
I stand here before you

00:14:00.222 --> 00:14:02.812
as somebody who has not done
that well for much of my life.

00:14:02.812 --> 00:14:06.722
And in fact one of my motivations

00:14:06.722 --> 00:14:08.052
to work on this subject area,

00:14:08.052 --> 00:14:09.902
one of the reasons I was compelled

00:14:09.902 --> 00:14:11.842
to go into it was because myself

00:14:11.842 --> 00:14:15.022
was experiencing a deep
well of loneliness.

00:14:15.022 --> 00:14:16.122
And it was very painful.

00:14:16.122 --> 00:14:18.652
And I felt that not only during childhood

00:14:18.652 --> 00:14:21.072
which was a real challenge
for me in elementary school

00:14:21.072 --> 00:14:23.142
but even during adulthood
when I was surgeon general

00:14:23.142 --> 00:14:25.702
and actually in the few
years that followed it.

00:14:25.702 --> 00:14:28.222
And what I realized I had done, Sal

00:14:28.222 --> 00:14:30.012
is I had always prioritized

00:14:30.012 --> 00:14:31.652
and cared about the people in my life.

00:14:31.652 --> 00:14:34.222
They were always you know,
from a stated perspective,

00:14:34.222 --> 00:14:36.062
my top concerns.

00:14:36.062 --> 00:14:38.072
But the way I was actually
living my lifestyle

00:14:38.072 --> 00:14:40.492
was not consistent with
those priorities, right?

00:14:40.492 --> 00:14:42.902
So there was a gap between
my stated priorities

00:14:42.902 --> 00:14:43.762
and my living priorities.

00:14:43.762 --> 00:14:46.752
Work was actually my top priority.

00:14:46.752 --> 00:14:48.882
And I came out of my time in government,

00:14:48.882 --> 00:14:50.972
after serving as surgeon
general for a few years,

00:14:50.972 --> 00:14:52.242
what I realized

00:14:52.242 --> 00:14:56.032
is that in an effort to
focus wholly on work,

00:14:56.032 --> 00:14:58.452
thinking hey, I don't know how
much time I have in office.

00:14:58.452 --> 00:15:00.792
I wanna get as much done as I can.

00:15:00.792 --> 00:15:02.412
And you know, contribute as much as I can

00:15:02.412 --> 00:15:04.042
to the public health.

00:15:04.042 --> 00:15:05.522
I made a critical mistake,

00:15:05.522 --> 00:15:06.632
which is that I assumed

00:15:06.632 --> 00:15:09.592
that I would be better able to do that

00:15:09.592 --> 00:15:10.892
if I gave all of my time,

00:15:10.892 --> 00:15:12.962
including that time
that I spent with family

00:15:12.962 --> 00:15:14.032
and with friends.

00:15:14.032 --> 00:15:16.042
And it turned that that hurt
me, like in the long run.

00:15:16.042 --> 00:15:19.332
It probably reduced how
much I could actually give

00:15:19.332 --> 00:15:23.352
to the job because it meant
that I was cutting myself off

00:15:23.352 --> 00:15:26.052
from one of the most important sources

00:15:26.052 --> 00:15:28.332
of fuel that we have in our lives.

00:15:28.332 --> 00:15:30.052
And that is our relationships
with one another.

00:15:30.052 --> 00:15:31.772
So here's what I do now.

00:15:31.772 --> 00:15:34.582
Now, when a friend calls,

00:15:34.582 --> 00:15:35.782
I pick up the phone,

00:15:35.782 --> 00:15:38.019
even if it's just for 10 seconds to say,

00:15:38.019 --> 00:15:40.102
"Hey, can I call you later?"

00:15:40.102 --> 00:15:43.582
Because just hearing
their voice is powerful.

00:15:43.582 --> 00:15:46.252
And is much more powerful
than declining the call

00:15:46.252 --> 00:15:47.819
and then sending them a text later saying,

00:15:47.819 --> 00:15:49.222
"Hey, can I call you back?"

00:15:49.222 --> 00:15:52.872
The second thing that I do is
I create space in my life now

00:15:52.872 --> 00:15:55.382
that is free from
distraction and technology.

00:15:55.382 --> 00:15:58.392
Often that's dinner time with the family

00:15:58.392 --> 00:16:00.782
and bedtime when me and my wife

00:16:00.782 --> 00:16:02.932
are putting our two children to bed.

00:16:02.932 --> 00:16:05.412
And it's time where we're
just focused on each other.

00:16:05.412 --> 00:16:07.312
And the third thing
that I'll mention to you

00:16:07.312 --> 00:16:10.372
is something that I formed
in my life called a moai.

00:16:10.372 --> 00:16:12.492
And a moai is something I
write about in the book.

00:16:12.492 --> 00:16:14.512
It's a Japanese concept

00:16:14.512 --> 00:16:18.092
actually that began in Okinawa
many, many generations ago.

00:16:18.092 --> 00:16:19.932
But the idea is that
they would take a group

00:16:19.932 --> 00:16:22.529
of young children, put
them together and say,

00:16:22.529 --> 00:16:24.922
"Now you are a group, you are a moai

00:16:24.922 --> 00:16:26.972
and you are responsible for each other.

00:16:26.972 --> 00:16:28.372
You have to look out for each other."

00:16:28.372 --> 00:16:30.962
And so they were making
an explicit commitment

00:16:30.962 --> 00:16:33.802
to one another to be there for each other.

00:16:33.802 --> 00:16:36.402
And what I realized in 2018,

00:16:36.402 --> 00:16:38.852
when I was at a fellowship
retreat with two close friends

00:16:38.852 --> 00:16:41.172
who I love dearly but rarely ever see

00:16:41.172 --> 00:16:44.282
is I realized that as lonely
as we were all feeling

00:16:44.282 --> 00:16:47.322
that we would not end
up truly reconnecting

00:16:47.322 --> 00:16:48.172
in the way that we needed

00:16:48.172 --> 00:16:49.922
unless we made an explicit commitment.

00:16:49.922 --> 00:16:52.892
So we said to each other,
why don't we form a moai?

00:16:52.892 --> 00:16:54.492
The commitments we'll make is once a month

00:16:54.492 --> 00:16:56.322
we'll video conference with each other.

00:16:56.322 --> 00:16:58.742
We'll make it a point to
be real with one another,

00:16:58.742 --> 00:17:00.502
to talk about this stuff that matters

00:17:00.502 --> 00:17:03.132
especially the things that
we don't talk about often

00:17:03.132 --> 00:17:04.082
with our friends,

00:17:04.082 --> 00:17:07.012
our relationships, our
finances and our health.

00:17:07.012 --> 00:17:08.412
And we also made it a point to say

00:17:08.412 --> 00:17:11.102
if something comes up
in between those calls

00:17:11.102 --> 00:17:12.162
we're gonna reach out to each other.

00:17:12.162 --> 00:17:13.222
We're gonna have a text thread

00:17:13.222 --> 00:17:14.382
and we'll just ping each other.

00:17:14.382 --> 00:17:16.092
And we can just get on the
phone for five minutes,

00:17:16.092 --> 00:17:18.312
you know, to talk through
a difficult situation

00:17:18.312 --> 00:17:19.692
we'll do that but we'll make a commitment

00:17:19.692 --> 00:17:21.182
to be there for one another.

00:17:21.182 --> 00:17:22.952
And in the last two years, Sal

00:17:22.952 --> 00:17:25.052
that moai has changed my life.

00:17:25.052 --> 00:17:28.912
It has been an incredibly
important anchor for me.

00:17:28.912 --> 00:17:31.732
And my two moai brothers, Sunny and Dave

00:17:31.732 --> 00:17:34.262
we helped each other
through so many challenges

00:17:34.262 --> 00:17:35.452
over the last few years.

00:17:35.452 --> 00:17:37.722
It wasn't that we weren't
friends before that

00:17:37.722 --> 00:17:38.972
but the difference, Sal

00:17:38.972 --> 00:17:41.932
was in taking these implicit
commitments around friendship

00:17:42.812 --> 00:17:43.962
and making them explicit.

00:17:43.962 --> 00:17:45.612
And that made all the difference.

00:17:46.592 --> 00:17:47.425
- I love that.

00:17:48.812 --> 00:17:49.842
I'm not joking.

00:17:49.842 --> 00:17:51.592
I think I'm already making the list

00:17:51.592 --> 00:17:54.502
of who are the two or three
people I need to join my moai.

00:17:54.502 --> 00:17:57.292
Because, you know, I'll share my own side.

00:17:57.292 --> 00:17:58.532
You know, I couldn't agree with you more

00:17:58.532 --> 00:18:02.092
about this delusion that many folks have

00:18:02.092 --> 00:18:04.942
that if you're already working 40 hours

00:18:04.942 --> 00:18:06.952
that you're gonna be able to
do your job that much better

00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:09.472
if you go to 50, 60, 70, 80 hours.

00:18:09.472 --> 00:18:10.352
It's just going to deplete you.

00:18:10.352 --> 00:18:13.202
I remember the first job I
had out of business school,

00:18:13.202 --> 00:18:14.035
it was at a hedge fund

00:18:14.035 --> 00:18:15.112
and I was ready to...

00:18:15.112 --> 00:18:17.062
I thought I would have
to work 70 or 80 hours.

00:18:17.062 --> 00:18:19.872
And my boss, it was
only a two person firm.

00:18:19.872 --> 00:18:22.502
He said, "Sal, our job
is to make good decisions

00:18:22.502 --> 00:18:24.042
not a bunch of bad decisions.

00:18:24.042 --> 00:18:26.312
So go home, don't think about work,

00:18:26.312 --> 00:18:27.462
connect with your family."

00:18:27.462 --> 00:18:30.182
And this was before, you know,
I had just gotten married.

00:18:30.182 --> 00:18:31.142
I didn't have kids at the time

00:18:31.142 --> 00:18:33.212
but that's what gave me the time and space

00:18:33.212 --> 00:18:34.482
to actually tutor Nadia

00:18:34.482 --> 00:18:36.852
and that led to all of Khan Academy.

00:18:36.852 --> 00:18:37.685
But more importantly,

00:18:37.685 --> 00:18:39.532
it actually did balance me out.

00:18:39.532 --> 00:18:42.082
And so I never forgot what Dan, Dan Wohl

00:18:42.082 --> 00:18:43.772
it was called Wohl Capital did then.

00:18:43.772 --> 00:18:47.042
And then I would say about five years ago

00:18:47.042 --> 00:18:50.892
you know, various stresses just
started to really get to me.

00:18:50.892 --> 00:18:53.875
And I started getting
claustrophobic in planes.

00:18:55.042 --> 00:18:56.462
And that's when I started, you know,

00:18:56.462 --> 00:18:59.132
just going down a similar journey

00:18:59.132 --> 00:19:01.032
although I'm learning
a lot from your journey

00:19:01.032 --> 00:19:02.892
of investing.

00:19:02.892 --> 00:19:04.592
That's when I started meditating regularly

00:19:04.592 --> 00:19:06.715
that's been transformational for me.

00:19:08.017 --> 00:19:09.032
And I didn't do a moai.

00:19:09.032 --> 00:19:10.282
I think I still need a moai

00:19:10.282 --> 00:19:12.772
but last year I started getting a group

00:19:12.772 --> 00:19:14.815
of broader friend circle of friends.

00:19:16.491 --> 00:19:17.452
We used to meet in the park

00:19:17.452 --> 00:19:19.642
but now we meet over video conference

00:19:19.642 --> 00:19:23.692
every week where we
discuss things about life.

00:19:23.692 --> 00:19:26.532
So there'll be a topic around parenting

00:19:26.532 --> 00:19:29.612
or tough love or perception versus reality

00:19:29.612 --> 00:19:32.402
but we'll dig into, you
know, what makes us tick.

00:19:32.402 --> 00:19:35.312
And there are these double
or triple click conversations

00:19:35.312 --> 00:19:37.292
versus what you normally
have at a gathering.

00:19:37.292 --> 00:19:39.012
But I really liked the moai concept

00:19:39.012 --> 00:19:40.362
with like a smaller group

00:19:40.362 --> 00:19:44.154
that you really are family in
the best sense of the word.

00:19:44.154 --> 00:19:46.332
(Khan laughs)

00:19:46.332 --> 00:19:47.502
- Yeah, 'cause Sal I'll tell you

00:19:47.502 --> 00:19:49.132
what really makes a difference here

00:19:49.132 --> 00:19:53.072
like in whether people
feel lonely or connected

00:19:53.072 --> 00:19:55.402
is really the quality
of those connections.

00:19:55.402 --> 00:19:57.202
And the thing is all of us,

00:19:57.202 --> 00:19:59.142
regardless of where we grew
up, what culture we're in

00:19:59.142 --> 00:20:01.652
or how old we are, we all
have three coordinates.

00:20:01.652 --> 00:20:05.452
We want to be seen and
understood for who we are,

00:20:05.452 --> 00:20:07.382
we want to know that we matter

00:20:07.382 --> 00:20:08.410
and we want to be loved.

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:10.522
Right that is true of all of us.

00:20:10.522 --> 00:20:12.782
And the thing is when you
can be in a relationship

00:20:12.782 --> 00:20:15.332
with someone, a friendship,
a romantic relationship

00:20:15.332 --> 00:20:18.175
where you feel like you
can show up as who you are.

00:20:19.395 --> 00:20:20.392
You don't have to put on errors,

00:20:20.392 --> 00:20:24.792
you can truly be yourself,
that's extraordinarily powerful.

00:20:24.792 --> 00:20:27.372
And you know, one of the great ways

00:20:27.372 --> 00:20:30.422
that we can actually create and experience

00:20:30.422 --> 00:20:33.152
that kind of connection
is actually by listening.

00:20:33.152 --> 00:20:36.742
And I mentioned this because
so much of our emphasis

00:20:36.742 --> 00:20:38.542
in modern day society is on action, right?

00:20:38.542 --> 00:20:39.612
We have a friend who has a problem

00:20:39.612 --> 00:20:42.062
we're thinking, okay,
boom how can I fix it?

00:20:42.062 --> 00:20:44.382
We've had somebody else who's
not even a friend, a stranger

00:20:44.382 --> 00:20:46.662
you know, who raises a concern
or says something wrong.

00:20:46.662 --> 00:20:47.932
We wanna know, okay,
how can we correct them?

00:20:47.932 --> 00:20:49.222
How can we get them the right answer?

00:20:49.222 --> 00:20:50.742
We're very action oriented.

00:20:50.742 --> 00:20:52.492
But in that, sometimes what gets lost

00:20:52.492 --> 00:20:53.792
is the power of listening.

00:20:53.792 --> 00:20:57.722
And when we're able to be
fully present with someone,

00:20:57.722 --> 00:21:00.782
when we're able to give them
the gift of our full attention,

00:21:00.782 --> 00:21:02.222
when they are able to then share

00:21:02.222 --> 00:21:05.012
and be open with us,

00:21:05.012 --> 00:21:06.752
that's an extraordinarily
powerful experience.

00:21:06.752 --> 00:21:09.752
And what we tell somebody
when we listen deeply to them

00:21:09.752 --> 00:21:13.482
is we tell them, I see
you, I understand you,

00:21:13.482 --> 00:21:15.642
you matter and you are loved.

00:21:15.642 --> 00:21:18.102
And that's why listening
is one of the most powerful

00:21:18.102 --> 00:21:20.995
forces we have in forging
strong connection.

00:21:22.702 --> 00:21:23.625
- No, I love that.

00:21:24.536 --> 00:21:25.369
It's super, super powerful.

00:21:25.369 --> 00:21:27.502
Again, you're exactly
right, you're exactly right.

00:21:27.502 --> 00:21:30.772
It doesn't seem like action
in the traditional sense

00:21:30.772 --> 00:21:32.732
but it actually often times leads

00:21:32.732 --> 00:21:35.832
to a much deeper relationship
than any form of action

00:21:35.832 --> 00:21:38.202
and actually, you know, better outcomes.

00:21:38.202 --> 00:21:39.112
There's a ton of questions.

00:21:39.112 --> 00:21:41.092
I've been monopolizing
you with all of mine

00:21:41.092 --> 00:21:42.282
and I have many more.

00:21:42.282 --> 00:21:44.102
But obviously as former surgeon general

00:21:44.102 --> 00:21:45.812
we're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:21:45.812 --> 00:21:47.612
I think everyone is super well versed.

00:21:47.612 --> 00:21:50.132
You know, we had Dr. Fauci on here on

00:21:50.132 --> 00:21:52.602
when we might get a vaccine
and the various therapy.

00:21:52.602 --> 00:21:54.212
So I think folks know about that

00:21:54.212 --> 00:21:56.122
although, I'm curious if
you have any perspectives

00:21:56.122 --> 00:21:56.955
that you haven't heard.

00:21:56.955 --> 00:21:59.572
But you know, there's a
question here from Facebook

00:21:59.572 --> 00:22:01.072
for Christopher McDade.

00:22:01.072 --> 00:22:02.062
With this pandemic,

00:22:02.062 --> 00:22:04.242
it's affected a lot of
people's mental health.

00:22:04.242 --> 00:22:06.082
How would you recommend dealing with this

00:22:06.082 --> 00:22:08.032
now that we are isolating ourselves?

00:22:08.032 --> 00:22:09.772
It goes back to the everything
we've been talking about.

00:22:09.772 --> 00:22:10.752
Loneliness is one thing

00:22:10.752 --> 00:22:14.222
but also depression and other
issues can be exacerbated.

00:22:14.222 --> 00:22:15.522
What are your views there?

00:22:16.522 --> 00:22:19.452
- Well first of all I wanna
thank him for his question.

00:22:19.452 --> 00:22:20.832
I mean, that's absolutely right.

00:22:20.832 --> 00:22:22.872
I think many people are struggling more

00:22:22.872 --> 00:22:24.272
during this pandemic.

00:22:24.272 --> 00:22:26.842
You know, keep in mind
human connection is healing.

00:22:26.842 --> 00:22:29.022
and in a time of great
stress what we typically do

00:22:29.022 --> 00:22:30.532
is we seek out more connection.

00:22:30.532 --> 00:22:32.052
We pick up the phone and call a friend.

00:22:32.052 --> 00:22:33.972
We go visit someone we care about.

00:22:33.972 --> 00:22:35.562
And the fact that we can't do that now

00:22:35.562 --> 00:22:37.182
I think has exacerbated that stress.

00:22:37.182 --> 00:22:40.462
It's why you see high levels
of depression and anxiety.

00:22:40.462 --> 00:22:43.862
It's why you see actually opioid overdoses

00:22:43.862 --> 00:22:46.712
and alcohol use also on the rise.

00:22:46.712 --> 00:22:49.002
But I do think that in this moment,

00:22:49.002 --> 00:22:51.822
we can actually find healing once again

00:22:51.822 --> 00:22:52.962
in social connection.

00:22:52.962 --> 00:22:55.042
We just have to look for it
in a slightly different way.

00:22:55.042 --> 00:22:57.662
So even though we can't for
example, go and visit a friend

00:22:57.662 --> 00:22:59.662
or get together you know, for dinner

00:22:59.662 --> 00:23:02.182
at a crowded restaurant
the same way we used to,

00:23:02.182 --> 00:23:06.482
we can actually see friends
in safe ways, outside.

00:23:06.482 --> 00:23:08.422
For example, if we're getting
together and wear masks

00:23:08.422 --> 00:23:10.832
and we're distanced from our
friends and we're outdoors.

00:23:10.832 --> 00:23:12.732
You know, we can create safe spaces

00:23:12.732 --> 00:23:13.992
to interact with one another.

00:23:13.992 --> 00:23:15.884
The other thing we can do

00:23:15.884 --> 00:23:18.122
is to keep in mind some of these ideas

00:23:18.122 --> 00:23:20.102
that Sal and I were talking about earlier.

00:23:20.102 --> 00:23:23.702
Around 15 minutes a day keeping
that to re interact with

00:23:23.702 --> 00:23:25.552
and to reach out to people you care about.

00:23:25.552 --> 00:23:27.842
Making sure you make that
quality of the time count

00:23:27.842 --> 00:23:29.282
by eliminating distraction.

00:23:29.282 --> 00:23:31.232
Looking for ways to help
people in your network

00:23:31.232 --> 00:23:32.372
whether that's a neighbor

00:23:32.372 --> 00:23:33.922
who might be struggling because

00:23:34.772 --> 00:23:36.382
they're worried to go to the grocery store

00:23:36.382 --> 00:23:38.092
or a coworker who might
be having a hard time

00:23:38.092 --> 00:23:40.272
teleworking and homeschooling their child.

00:23:40.272 --> 00:23:41.872
And maybe you just dropping off some food

00:23:41.872 --> 00:23:43.112
will make all the difference.

00:23:43.112 --> 00:23:45.132
These are small but powerful ways to serve

00:23:45.132 --> 00:23:47.582
that can actually help boost our mood

00:23:47.582 --> 00:23:49.602
as well as strengthen our connection.

00:23:49.602 --> 00:23:51.252
So the last thing I'll just mention here

00:23:51.252 --> 00:23:53.092
because it's counterintuitive

00:23:53.092 --> 00:23:54.959
is actually the importance of solitude.

00:23:54.959 --> 00:23:55.792
And you might think,

00:23:55.792 --> 00:23:57.332
gosh if you're looking
for human connection,

00:23:57.332 --> 00:23:58.472
why solitude important?

00:23:58.472 --> 00:23:59.342
Isn't that the opposite?

00:23:59.342 --> 00:24:00.652
Isn't that being alone?

00:24:00.652 --> 00:24:04.332
Well, solitude is about
being physically alone

00:24:04.332 --> 00:24:08.212
but it's actually a
state of calm aloneness

00:24:08.212 --> 00:24:09.782
and joyful aloneness.

00:24:09.782 --> 00:24:11.592
And it's very different from loneliness

00:24:11.592 --> 00:24:13.132
which is a subjective state

00:24:13.132 --> 00:24:16.222
where I feel pained about
my lack of connection.

00:24:16.222 --> 00:24:17.442
And I feel the connections I need

00:24:17.442 --> 00:24:19.222
are greater than the connections I have.

00:24:19.222 --> 00:24:22.102
And the reason we all need
some solitude in our lives,

00:24:22.102 --> 00:24:24.702
the reason we have to actually recapture

00:24:24.702 --> 00:24:26.732
our ability to be comfortable

00:24:26.732 --> 00:24:28.802
being alone for some periods of time

00:24:28.802 --> 00:24:30.962
is because it is in
those moments of solitude

00:24:30.962 --> 00:24:33.632
that we allow the noise
around us to settle.

00:24:33.632 --> 00:24:36.142
It's when we recenter
and reground ourselves.

00:24:36.142 --> 00:24:37.812
It's when we reflect.

00:24:37.812 --> 00:24:40.582
And when we allow ourselves
to just be in a world

00:24:40.582 --> 00:24:42.952
that is constantly
moving faster and faster.

00:24:42.952 --> 00:24:45.412
And when we can approach other people

00:24:45.412 --> 00:24:48.332
from a place of being more
grounded and more centered.

00:24:48.332 --> 00:24:51.022
We're more able to listen deeply to them.

00:24:51.022 --> 00:24:53.412
We're more able to be present ourselves

00:24:53.412 --> 00:24:56.602
and to share ourselves rather
than constantly worrying

00:24:56.602 --> 00:24:58.482
about what the other person is saying.

00:24:58.482 --> 00:25:01.022
You know, I say this
knowing that for many people

00:25:01.022 --> 00:25:03.372
that finding an hour or
two to meditate every day

00:25:03.372 --> 00:25:06.172
for example, might be way too much time.

00:25:06.172 --> 00:25:09.332
But solitude can come in many
shapes and forms and sizes.

00:25:09.332 --> 00:25:11.052
It can look like meditation.

00:25:11.052 --> 00:25:13.322
It can look like just
time where you're sitting

00:25:13.322 --> 00:25:14.162
on your front porch

00:25:14.162 --> 00:25:16.952
and letting the breeze
blow against your face.

00:25:16.952 --> 00:25:18.212
It could be a walk in nature.

00:25:18.212 --> 00:25:19.332
It could be time in prayer.

00:25:19.332 --> 00:25:21.302
It could be time spent listening to music

00:25:21.302 --> 00:25:23.482
that soothes you.

00:25:23.482 --> 00:25:25.792
But whatever it is and
for however long it is,

00:25:25.792 --> 00:25:27.952
we need those times of solitude.

00:25:27.952 --> 00:25:29.292
You know, I'll leave you with this wisdom

00:25:29.292 --> 00:25:32.052
from my nephrology
professor in medical school.

00:25:32.052 --> 00:25:34.942
This is one of my dearest mentors.

00:25:34.942 --> 00:25:36.652
Her name is Dr. Peggy Bia.

00:25:36.652 --> 00:25:39.312
And when I used to think I'm too busy,

00:25:39.312 --> 00:25:41.782
you know to create time
for solitude in my life,

00:25:41.782 --> 00:25:42.615
I would think of her.

00:25:42.615 --> 00:25:44.062
'Cause she was a busy mom,

00:25:44.062 --> 00:25:47.632
teacher, doctor and
hospital administrator.

00:25:47.632 --> 00:25:48.932
But what she would do

00:25:48.932 --> 00:25:51.242
even though she didn't have
much in the way of time,

00:25:51.242 --> 00:25:52.602
was recognizing how important

00:25:52.602 --> 00:25:54.152
those moments of solitude were.

00:25:55.032 --> 00:25:57.192
Whenever she was about to
walk into a patient's room,

00:25:57.192 --> 00:25:59.622
she would go to the
sink to wash her hands.

00:25:59.622 --> 00:26:01.122
And for 20 seconds

00:26:01.122 --> 00:26:04.592
she would just allow that warm
water to run over her hands.

00:26:04.592 --> 00:26:06.842
And she would take a deep breath

00:26:06.842 --> 00:26:09.872
and she would remember what
she had to be grateful for

00:26:09.872 --> 00:26:10.912
that day.

00:26:10.912 --> 00:26:13.212
Maybe it was the hug
that her kids gave her

00:26:13.212 --> 00:26:14.862
when she was leaving the house.

00:26:14.862 --> 00:26:16.512
Maybe it was a medical student

00:26:16.512 --> 00:26:18.272
for whom an idea finally clicked

00:26:18.272 --> 00:26:20.212
when she was teaching him that morning.

00:26:20.212 --> 00:26:22.452
Maybe it was the
opportunity to participate

00:26:22.452 --> 00:26:24.062
in the care of the patient

00:26:24.062 --> 00:26:26.632
whose room she was about to walk into.

00:26:26.632 --> 00:26:27.842
And then taking another deep breath

00:26:27.842 --> 00:26:30.482
she would turn the
water off, dry her hands

00:26:30.482 --> 00:26:32.562
and walk into that patient's room

00:26:32.562 --> 00:26:35.072
feeling more grounded, more centered

00:26:35.072 --> 00:26:39.742
more clear about who she
wanted to be than before.

00:26:39.742 --> 00:26:43.212
And that is the power of
20 seconds of solitude.

00:26:43.212 --> 00:26:45.352
And we all have 20 seconds in our life

00:26:45.352 --> 00:26:47.092
but it's important for us is to remember

00:26:47.092 --> 00:26:49.082
that power of that solitude.

00:26:49.082 --> 00:26:50.102
And moments like this

00:26:50.102 --> 00:26:52.362
when the world is turning around so fast,

00:26:52.362 --> 00:26:54.032
when there's so much uncertainty

00:26:54.032 --> 00:26:55.892
we need to anchor ourselves.

00:26:55.892 --> 00:26:59.332
And moments of solitude, our
relationships with one another

00:26:59.332 --> 00:27:01.232
are some of our most powerful anchors.

00:27:03.182 --> 00:27:04.992
- Vivek, I gotta tell you,

00:27:04.992 --> 00:27:07.732
you have a future in guided meditation.

00:27:07.732 --> 00:27:10.239
Like I could just take snippets (laughs)

00:27:10.239 --> 00:27:11.557
of what you just said

00:27:11.557 --> 00:27:12.952
and I was feeling it.

00:27:12.952 --> 00:27:13.865
I'm not kidding.

00:27:15.068 --> 00:27:16.732
I think there's something very powerful

00:27:16.732 --> 00:27:18.932
in even just your answer itself.

00:27:18.932 --> 00:27:20.272
About you're right.

00:27:20.272 --> 00:27:22.882
I felt that warm water over my hands

00:27:22.882 --> 00:27:25.402
and I was like yes I have
to feel the gratitude.

00:27:25.402 --> 00:27:26.575
But you're so right.

00:27:28.312 --> 00:27:30.982
You know a lot of people
think that I'm extroverted

00:27:30.982 --> 00:27:32.362
'cause I can sometimes appear

00:27:32.362 --> 00:27:33.912
but I'm very much an introvert.

00:27:35.372 --> 00:27:37.042
The notion is where you get your energy

00:27:37.042 --> 00:27:39.232
and I need that solitude
but you're exactly right.

00:27:39.232 --> 00:27:41.312
Solitude versus loneliness

00:27:43.437 --> 00:27:44.632
are not the same thing.

00:27:44.632 --> 00:27:47.182
You could be lonely in
a crowd as you said,

00:27:47.182 --> 00:27:49.292
on a college campus, in a room at a party,

00:27:49.292 --> 00:27:50.412
you can feel very lonely.

00:27:50.412 --> 00:27:53.332
In fact, that's often
when I feel the loneliness

00:27:53.332 --> 00:27:57.222
the most lonely versus when I'm alone

00:27:57.222 --> 00:27:59.332
or when I'm with a very
small group of folks

00:27:59.332 --> 00:28:01.712
but it's a super powerful idea.

00:28:01.712 --> 00:28:03.402
You know, just one more question.

00:28:03.402 --> 00:28:04.532
I know we're limited on time.

00:28:04.532 --> 00:28:05.952
There's so much to talk about here.

00:28:05.952 --> 00:28:07.762
Facebook, Michael Graves,

00:28:07.762 --> 00:28:10.862
how profoundly does
rejection in a general sense

00:28:10.862 --> 00:28:12.862
affect one's loneliness slash depression?

00:28:12.862 --> 00:28:15.185
Is there a way to mitigate the feeling?

00:28:17.332 --> 00:28:21.202
- Well, I think rejection is
so powerful and so damaging

00:28:21.202 --> 00:28:22.742
because it gets at the core

00:28:22.742 --> 00:28:25.032
of whether we have worth as human beings.

00:28:25.032 --> 00:28:26.622
When we feel rejected,

00:28:26.622 --> 00:28:29.792
what we're hearing from someone else

00:28:29.792 --> 00:28:31.525
is that you don't have value,

00:28:32.382 --> 00:28:33.942
that you're not worthy.

00:28:33.942 --> 00:28:37.722
And this is especially
challenging for young people

00:28:37.722 --> 00:28:39.832
whose identity is developing.

00:28:39.832 --> 00:28:42.612
Now, this doesn't mean that we, you know,

00:28:42.612 --> 00:28:45.272
can go through life and
figure out how to deal

00:28:45.272 --> 00:28:46.992
with disappointment, right?

00:28:46.992 --> 00:28:49.712
But there's a difference
between disappointment

00:28:49.712 --> 00:28:51.732
and feeling rejected, right?

00:28:51.732 --> 00:28:53.532
Disappointment is you know,

00:28:53.532 --> 00:28:55.312
I wanted something to
work on in a certain way

00:28:55.312 --> 00:28:56.902
and it didn't.

00:28:56.902 --> 00:29:00.682
Feeling rejected is feeling,
gosh, I'm not worthy.

00:29:00.682 --> 00:29:02.442
I'm not valuable.

00:29:02.442 --> 00:29:05.122
That's what, you know, the
situation is telling me.

00:29:05.122 --> 00:29:08.392
And we, I think can raise our children

00:29:08.392 --> 00:29:10.222
to deal with disappointment.

00:29:10.222 --> 00:29:11.532
But what we have to do

00:29:11.532 --> 00:29:12.892
what's so important for all of us,

00:29:12.892 --> 00:29:15.172
whether we're about kids or adults,

00:29:15.172 --> 00:29:19.022
is to know where our worth
fundamentally comes from.

00:29:19.022 --> 00:29:20.762
'Cause that should be a deep root

00:29:21.602 --> 00:29:25.212
that we cultivate and build and nurture

00:29:25.212 --> 00:29:26.592
over the course of our lives.

00:29:26.592 --> 00:29:30.732
It should be a root that's
not shaken by circumstance.

00:29:30.732 --> 00:29:31.882
And we all have challenges.

00:29:31.882 --> 00:29:34.822
Like I struggle with
my own self-worth a lot

00:29:34.822 --> 00:29:36.352
and have over many years.

00:29:36.352 --> 00:29:40.202
It's a constant effort
for me to remind myself

00:29:40.202 --> 00:29:41.542
during low points in my life

00:29:41.542 --> 00:29:43.562
where my value truly comes from.

00:29:43.562 --> 00:29:46.692
But this is actually where
friendship is so important

00:29:46.692 --> 00:29:49.892
'cause when I was in
college and I asked somebody

00:29:49.892 --> 00:29:51.532
actually a college classmates

00:29:51.532 --> 00:29:53.412
on of these late night
philosophical conversations

00:29:53.412 --> 00:29:55.002
that you tend to have in college.

00:29:55.002 --> 00:29:57.412
I said, "Hey, what do
you think a friend is?"

00:29:57.412 --> 00:29:58.532
And he paused for a moment

00:29:58.532 --> 00:30:00.822
and he said, "A friend is
someone who reminds you

00:30:00.822 --> 00:30:03.352
of who you are when you forget."

00:30:03.352 --> 00:30:06.052
And I've always remembered
that definition over the years

00:30:06.052 --> 00:30:08.482
that a friend is someone who reminds you

00:30:08.482 --> 00:30:10.412
of who you are when you forget.

00:30:10.412 --> 00:30:12.972
Because we all need mirrors like that.

00:30:12.972 --> 00:30:15.522
People who can show us not
just who we think we are

00:30:15.522 --> 00:30:16.572
but who we truly are.

00:30:16.572 --> 00:30:19.782
Who can remind us of what
we bring to the world

00:30:19.782 --> 00:30:20.615
that's so special.

00:30:20.615 --> 00:30:21.532
Maybe it's our humor.

00:30:21.532 --> 00:30:22.482
Maybe it's our insight.

00:30:22.482 --> 00:30:25.082
Maybe it's our compassion or a kindness.

00:30:25.082 --> 00:30:28.702
Maybe it's all of those things
but we need those reminders.

00:30:28.702 --> 00:30:30.492
You know, Sal I'll just say, you know,

00:30:30.492 --> 00:30:33.182
that this whole topic
that we've been discussing

00:30:33.182 --> 00:30:35.792
this subject around
connection and mental health

00:30:35.792 --> 00:30:36.675
and loneliness.

00:30:37.632 --> 00:30:39.912
This whole book that I
wrote on the subject.

00:30:39.912 --> 00:30:43.292
While ostensibly this is
about the topic of connection,

00:30:43.292 --> 00:30:45.912
what it's truly about and
what I came to realize

00:30:45.912 --> 00:30:49.012
toward the end of this writing
is it's really about love.

00:30:49.012 --> 00:30:51.652
It's about the question
of how do we build lives

00:30:51.652 --> 00:30:53.622
and truly a society that's more grounded

00:30:53.622 --> 00:30:56.062
in human connection but really in love?

00:30:56.062 --> 00:30:58.362
Because the two forces
that are fundamentally

00:30:59.642 --> 00:31:01.242
driving us in our actions

00:31:01.242 --> 00:31:02.862
and you can see this
on display in the world

00:31:02.862 --> 00:31:04.152
are love and fear.

00:31:04.152 --> 00:31:06.872
And the fear manifests a lot as anger,

00:31:06.872 --> 00:31:09.962
as insecurity, as jealousy, as rage.

00:31:09.962 --> 00:31:11.932
But we see the love manifesting as well

00:31:11.932 --> 00:31:16.312
as kindness, as compassion,
as generosity, as concern.

00:31:16.312 --> 00:31:18.732
But all of us have the ability

00:31:18.732 --> 00:31:22.322
and the capacity to be
driven by love or fear.

00:31:22.322 --> 00:31:25.162
And the question that I
think about most often

00:31:25.162 --> 00:31:26.422
when I look at my own kids

00:31:26.422 --> 00:31:29.422
and I think about what the
world will look like for them,

00:31:29.422 --> 00:31:32.562
is I think that the world will
be more hospitable to them.

00:31:32.562 --> 00:31:36.242
More encouraging, more
supportive, more nurturing

00:31:36.242 --> 00:31:39.402
if you and I and everyone out there

00:31:39.402 --> 00:31:42.332
does what we can to tip
the balance in the world

00:31:42.332 --> 00:31:44.162
away from fear and toward love.

00:31:44.162 --> 00:31:46.622
And to do that by the
words we choose to speak,

00:31:46.622 --> 00:31:48.322
by how we treat other people,

00:31:48.322 --> 00:31:50.102
by the issues we choose to work on

00:31:50.102 --> 00:31:53.632
as well as the topics that we
choose to raise our voice on

00:31:53.632 --> 00:31:56.002
and stand up for in the public square.

00:31:56.002 --> 00:31:58.482
That's fundamentally what this is about.

00:31:58.482 --> 00:32:01.692
And when I forget, you know, at times

00:32:01.692 --> 00:32:03.605
just how powerful that love can be.

00:32:04.882 --> 00:32:09.882
What I think about is
this ten second exercise

00:32:09.972 --> 00:32:14.332
that Fred Rogers actually
he was famous for doing

00:32:14.332 --> 00:32:15.902
during his talks.

00:32:15.902 --> 00:32:17.042
And if you have 10 seconds,

00:32:17.042 --> 00:32:19.982
I'll just walk you and our
listeners through it right now.

00:32:19.982 --> 00:32:21.782
If you take your hand

00:32:21.782 --> 00:32:24.802
and you just put it on your
heart and you close your eyes

00:32:26.002 --> 00:32:27.205
and take a deep breath.

00:32:28.632 --> 00:32:30.832
And just think about
the people in your life

00:32:31.882 --> 00:32:33.232
who have been there for you

00:32:34.182 --> 00:32:36.375
during your highs and your lows.

00:32:37.512 --> 00:32:40.435
Think about the people
who have supported you,

00:32:41.692 --> 00:32:43.132
who have taken care of you,

00:32:43.132 --> 00:32:45.392
who have listened to you,

00:32:45.392 --> 00:32:50.392
who have understood you even
at times when you weren't sure

00:32:50.952 --> 00:32:54.085
if you were worthy of friendship and love.

00:32:55.502 --> 00:32:57.362
Think about the people you turn to

00:32:57.362 --> 00:33:00.905
in moments of pain and in moments of joy.

00:33:02.142 --> 00:33:06.022
And feel their love just washing over you

00:33:07.422 --> 00:33:11.205
filling you with peace,
filling you with happiness,

00:33:12.472 --> 00:33:17.135
filling you with joy and open your eyes.

00:33:19.042 --> 00:33:21.375
And what you felt in those 10 seconds.

00:33:22.547 --> 00:33:24.692
That was the power of love.

00:33:24.692 --> 00:33:27.472
And that love is most directly experienced

00:33:27.472 --> 00:33:30.282
through our relationships
with other people.

00:33:30.282 --> 00:33:32.702
And as a doctor who has
written prescriptions

00:33:32.702 --> 00:33:34.992
for many, many medicines over the years.

00:33:34.992 --> 00:33:36.682
Some incredibly powerful ones

00:33:36.682 --> 00:33:39.462
that are the result of amazing science.

00:33:39.462 --> 00:33:43.602
I will tell you that there
is no force more powerful.

00:33:43.602 --> 00:33:47.932
There is no medicine that
is more healing than love.

00:33:47.932 --> 00:33:50.262
And we all have the ability

00:33:50.262 --> 00:33:53.122
to heal because we all
have the ability to love.

00:33:53.122 --> 00:33:56.812
You don't need an MD or
an RN behind your name

00:33:56.812 --> 00:33:59.332
in order to participate
in that healing process

00:33:59.332 --> 00:34:03.062
that I think is at the heart
of what we need to do right now

00:34:03.062 --> 00:34:05.962
to help strengthen our
families, our communities

00:34:05.962 --> 00:34:07.212
and ultimately our world.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:11.412
- Well thank you for, I mean,
that was incredibly powerful.

00:34:11.412 --> 00:34:12.982
I don't wanna ruin what you just said.

00:34:12.982 --> 00:34:14.752
I mean, you should just
drop the mic at that point

00:34:14.752 --> 00:34:17.242
because there's so much in there.

00:34:17.242 --> 00:34:19.092
But I think what's powerful
about what you just said

00:34:19.092 --> 00:34:23.772
and even the exercise is you'll
hear even in cheesy movies

00:34:23.772 --> 00:34:26.332
love is what runs the universe or whatever

00:34:26.332 --> 00:34:27.492
but you're like, what do they really mean?

00:34:27.492 --> 00:34:30.582
But I think your definition
of it is a very tangible one.

00:34:30.582 --> 00:34:32.022
And it actually it was a bit of an aha

00:34:32.022 --> 00:34:33.562
that you are right.

00:34:33.562 --> 00:34:34.642
At the end of the day,

00:34:34.642 --> 00:34:38.032
almost every action is
really driven by love.

00:34:38.032 --> 00:34:42.082
Every thought is really
driven by either love or fear.

00:34:42.082 --> 00:34:44.012
And that even when you said that,

00:34:44.012 --> 00:34:45.832
I started thinking about
some of my own thoughts

00:34:45.832 --> 00:34:47.122
or my own actions

00:34:47.122 --> 00:34:49.242
and immediately I started
being able to classify them.

00:34:49.242 --> 00:34:51.602
No that's a fear one, that's a love one.

00:34:51.602 --> 00:34:54.162
And maybe that fear one, I
should do something different

00:34:54.162 --> 00:34:56.062
because that's not a great motivation.

00:34:57.343 --> 00:34:58.745
What you're saying is so powerful.

00:35:02.822 --> 00:35:06.474
You should be the meditation
general or something like that.

00:35:06.474 --> 00:35:08.012
(Sal laughs)

00:35:08.012 --> 00:35:11.052
And maybe a future administration
they will introduce

00:35:11.052 --> 00:35:13.482
some type of mindfulness for the country.

00:35:13.482 --> 00:35:15.312
I'm not joking, I wasn't kidding

00:35:15.312 --> 00:35:17.862
even that last exercise
you're really good at this.

00:35:19.944 --> 00:35:20.777
And I think you're good at it

00:35:20.777 --> 00:35:22.692
because it comes from a
place of genuine authenticity

00:35:22.692 --> 00:35:25.802
and love which is a very,
very powerful thing.

00:35:25.802 --> 00:35:27.892
So, you know, Vivek, thanks so much.

00:35:27.892 --> 00:35:28.882
I know we're all out of time.

00:35:28.882 --> 00:35:29.972
We went actually way over time.

00:35:29.972 --> 00:35:32.482
This was really powerful
but thanks so much for this.

00:35:32.482 --> 00:35:35.242
And I encourage everyone
to check out "Together"

00:35:35.242 --> 00:35:38.722
and just try to figure out
ways to lead a healthier,

00:35:38.722 --> 00:35:41.815
more robust, together, less lonely life.

00:35:42.892 --> 00:35:43.902
- Well, thanks so much Sal.

00:35:43.902 --> 00:35:46.068
It was so nice to be with you

00:35:46.068 --> 00:35:47.712
and thanks for your
interest in this subject

00:35:47.712 --> 00:35:48.812
and for everything you're doing

00:35:48.812 --> 00:35:51.352
to help support families
who are in this time.

00:35:51.352 --> 00:35:54.252
I must tell you and I wanna
encourage all of your listeners

00:35:54.252 --> 00:35:57.702
to support Khan Academy and
to contribute what you can.

00:35:57.702 --> 00:36:00.172
And, you know, I'm a beneficiary myself

00:36:00.172 --> 00:36:02.682
because my two kids
they love Khan Academy,

00:36:02.682 --> 00:36:03.952
especially Khan Academy Kids.

00:36:03.952 --> 00:36:06.202
And they're constantly
asking if they can be on it.

00:36:06.202 --> 00:36:08.702
So you've provided a reward for our kids.

00:36:08.702 --> 00:36:11.302
So now if we want them to brush
their teeth or take a bath

00:36:11.302 --> 00:36:13.012
we say, "Okay, you can
watch Khan Academy kids

00:36:13.012 --> 00:36:14.402
for a few minutes, if
you brush your teeth."

00:36:14.402 --> 00:36:18.496
So thank you Sal for the
parenting help. (laughs)

00:36:18.496 --> 00:36:19.329
- Good to hear that.

00:36:19.329 --> 00:36:21.352
No, thank you so much.

00:36:21.352 --> 00:36:22.185
- Of course.

00:36:22.185 --> 00:36:23.185
Take care then, be well.

00:36:24.032 --> 00:36:25.522
- See you.

00:36:25.522 --> 00:36:28.022
Well thanks everyone for joining.

00:36:28.022 --> 00:36:29.502
Yeah, that was a great conversation.

00:36:29.502 --> 00:36:32.642
And I wasn't joking with Vivek

00:36:32.642 --> 00:36:35.302
that he should do some
whatever you wanna call them

00:36:35.302 --> 00:36:38.042
meditations or guided whatever.

00:36:38.042 --> 00:36:41.022
But I thought not only did he
have some really good insights

00:36:41.022 --> 00:36:42.752
about I think a very important issue

00:36:42.752 --> 00:36:45.192
that goes way overlooked for most of us,

00:36:45.192 --> 00:36:50.192
but also very tangible
ways of working through it.

00:36:50.342 --> 00:36:51.662
So thanks for joining

00:36:51.662 --> 00:36:55.322
the next of our live
stream is going to be...

00:36:55.322 --> 00:36:57.422
We're gonna have not as good of a guest.

00:36:57.422 --> 00:37:00.632
It's going to be me. (laughs)

00:37:00.632 --> 00:37:02.812
You can ask me anything.

00:37:02.812 --> 00:37:05.732
So see you tomorrow at noon, pacific time.

00:37:05.732 --> 00:37:06.632
Talk to you later.

